<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philadelphia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philadelphia is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, on the Delaware river in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> founded it in 1682 as the capital of his Quaker colony, on land earlier settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and Swedes. It was the largest city of colonial America, the meeting place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a>, which adopted the Declaration of Independence there in 1776, and the seat of the Constitutional Convention of 1787.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philadelphia.jpg" alt="Philadelphia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 颐园居 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philadelphia_skyline_20240528_(cropped_2-1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1787 Alexander Hamilton told the Constitutional Convention in <b>Philadelphia</b> that the American executive should be an elected monarch serving during “good behaviour”, that is, for life unless impeached, strong enough to resist foreign corruption and yet controlled enough to prevent tyranny at home.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Constitutional Convention in <b>Philadelphia</b> in 1787 the Virginia Plan proposed an executive chosen by the legislature.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Many were settled in the Faiyum, which the Ptolemies drained and colonised with Greeks and Egyptians, founding villages such as <b>Philadelphia</b> and Karanis.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1863 the Supervisory Committee for Recruiting Colored Regiments in <b>Philadelphia</b> printed it as a broadside.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Others claimed the tune, among them William Steffe, who said he had written a song for a <b>Philadelphia</b> fire company in 1855 or 1856, and Thomas Brigham Bishop (1835–1905); none of them could have composed a tune printed decades before they were born.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li><li><blockquote>…long thought to descend from &quot;I'll Overcome Some Day&quot;, a hymn by the Reverend Charles Albert Tindley of <b>Philadelphia</b>, a Methodist Episcopal minister, first published in 1901, written in an idiom rooted in African American folk traditions for congregations many of whom had been freed from slavery a…</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a></li></ul></section>
